    "result_text": "Tests green (2/2), `BUILD SUCCESS`. Here's how I addressed each finding.\n\n**1. Duplicated postcode normalization \u2014 fixed.** Extracted `Household.normalizePostcode(String)` as the single definition, routed `Household.key` through it, and replaced `FlagPossibleDuplicate`'s inline `normalize(...)` (now deleted) with calls to it. Soft-match and household membership now share one postcode-normalization rule.\n\n**2. SHA-256-hex implemented twice (three times, actually) \u2014 fixed.** Added `model.Sha256.hex(String)` as the single digest-to-lowercase-hex routine (with the one `NoSuchAlgorithmException \u2192 IllegalStateException` handler). Callers now take what they need from it:\n- `Owner.identityKey` \u2192 full 64-hex (removed `Owner.sha256Hex` and its now-unused imports).\n- `Household.id` \u2192 `\"H-\" + Sha256.hex(...).substring(0, 12)`.\n- `AssignCustomerCode.hash8` \u2192 `Sha256.hex(...).substring(0, 8).toUpperCase(...)` \u2014 not flagged, but it was a third copy of the same pattern, so folding it in keeps \"one place this lives\" honest. All three produce byte-identical outputs to before (customer codes, household ids, identity keys unchanged).\n\nI put `Sha256` in the `model` package (alongside the existing `TelephoneFormatter` utility) rather than `util`, because `util` already depends on `model` (`EntityUtils \u2192 BaseEntity`); placing it in `util` would have created a `model`\u2194`util` package cycle, whereas `model` keeps the dependency one-directional.\n\n**Minor (soundex on `Owner`) \u2014 left as-is, with reasoning.** The reviewer flagged this as non-blocking and defensible. There's no existing text/normalization util (the `util` package holds only `EntityUtils`, an entity lookup), and `soundex` exists solely to feed `Owner.identityKey`, which also lives on `Owner`. Moving just `soundex` out while `identityKey` stays would split one cohesive derivation across two classes for no real gain, so I left it co-located with its only caller.\n\nNo acceptance behaviour changed \u2014 all derived values are computed identically; this was a pure structural de-duplication.",
