    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe change is largely coherent \u2014 retiring `CheckHouseholdUnique`/`HouseholdDuplicateException` in favour of `CheckIdentityUnique`/`DuplicateIdentityException`, renaming the handler and YAML, and moving `soundex`/`identityKey` onto `Owner` alongside the existing key logic are all natural placements. But two duplication issues work against this codebase's stated \"single source of truth / formats cannot drift\" discipline:\n\n1. **Postcode normalization is now duplicated.** `FlagPossibleDuplicate.normalize(postcode)` (`FlagPossibleDuplicate.java:55-57`) reimplements, inline, the exact trim-only postcode normalization that `Household.key` already owns (`Household.java:47`: `(postcode == null) ? \"\" : postcode.trim()`). `Household` is explicitly documented as the single source of truth for the (lastName, postcode) pairing, and the soft-match here keys on postcode too. Having a second, independent copy is precisely the drift this codebase guards against. Extract/reuse a shared postcode-normalization helper (or route through `Household`) so the soft-match and household membership can't diverge if the rule changes.\n\n2. **SHA-256-hex is now implemented twice.** `Owner.sha256Hex` (new, full 64-hex) and `Household.id` (`Household.java:72-85`, inline `MessageDigest` + `%02x` loop, first 12 hex with an `H-` prefix) are near-identical digest-to-hex routines with the same `NoSuchAlgorithmException` \u2192 `IllegalStateException` handling. Consider a single shared hex-digest helper that both call, so there's one place this lives.\n\nMinor (not blocking): `Owner.soundex`/`soundexDigit` are a general-purpose string algorithm hung on the `Owner` entity. It's defensible since it feeds `identityKey` (also on `Owner`), but if a shared text/normalization util already exists it would be a more natural home and would keep `Owner` from accreting string-algorithm helpers. The duplication items above are the ones worth addressing.",
